WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.260
All right, let's talk about computer architecture.

2
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.500
Now, what is computer architecture?

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.700
You've probably heard about architecture before

4
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.580
when people are talking about skyscrapers

5
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:15.460
and hospitals and museums and stuff like that.

6
00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:19.180
Well, computer architecture is actually pretty simple.

7
00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:20.820
There's lots of different kinds of buildings,

8
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.300
lots of different kinds of computers,

9
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.340
and you wouldn't architect a home

10
00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:27.100
the same way you wouldn't architect a car wash,

11
00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.500
and you wouldn't architect a museum,

12
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.860
the same way you do our restaurant.

13
00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:32.460
Well, there's different types of computers.

14
00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.660
You wouldn't architect them exactly the same either

15
00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.180
because each one has its own specific set of needs

16
00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.540
and purposes, just like the buildings.

17
00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:42.620
So, there's certain things you need to take into consideration

18
00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.460
when thinking about computer architecture.

19
00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.900
It's these set of rules and methods

20
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.540
that describe the functionality, organization,

21
00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:53.140
and implementation of the computer system.

22
00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.740
Now, this includes hardware, software,

23
00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.420
and everything in between and all around it.

24
00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:00.100
Let's talk about hardware.

25
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:01.340
When we talk about hardware,

26
00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:04.380
we're talking about the stuff you can physically see and touch.

27
00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:08.260
Stuff like CPUs, memory, hard drives, network cards,

28
00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.260
IO adapters, the graphical processing unit,

29
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:12.660
all that kind of stuff.

30
00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.860
The stuff that you can pick up, take a look at

31
00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.020
and shove back into the computer.

32
00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.420
Don't shove it back into the computer.

33
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:19.580
Place it gently.

34
00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:20.740
We're talking about software.

35
00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.100
That's the stuff that we can't see.

36
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.980
That stuff like programs that are written in code,

37
00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:28.460
operating systems, built-in programs like Notepad,

38
00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:30.500
applications you're right, libraries,

39
00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.620
ones you download, tools, utilities, programs,

40
00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:35.100
all sorts of stuff.

41
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.980
And you can think about the operating system

42
00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:38.740
as the primary program,

43
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:40.780
and everything else just goes through it.

44
00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:43.380
So there are instances when you're gonna write software

45
00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.820
that sits directly on top of the hardware

46
00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:47.380
that doesn't use the operating system.

47
00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.540
Those do exist, but most of the time,

48
00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.460
the operating system is there in the middle,

49
00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.020
making sure not only this stuff is easier to write,

50
00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:56.140
but that it's gonna run safer as well.

51
00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:57.620
All right, let's get into these computer

52
00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:59.300
architecture components.

53
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.780
Earlier I talked about how architecture computer

54
00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:04.220
is a lot like the architecture of a building.

55
00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.540
Lots of different buildings, lots of different computers.

56
00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:10.100
Now a building typically has a couple of things in common.

57
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.260
It's got walls, it's got a roof, got floor,

58
00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.860
hallway, it probably has an electrical system,

59
00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.260
probably plumbing, windows, doors.

60
00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:20.780
You can see there's a lot of stuff to think about there.

61
00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:24.100
In computer architecture, you've got a series of components.

62
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:26.740
And the architecture is all about how they are designed

63
00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.020
to work well with another and fit together

64
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:32.660
so that it performs as a bank or as a museum again.

65
00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:35.980
And we can kind of see this in computer architecture as well.

66
00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.020
And there's this split between the hardware

67
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:39.460
and the software side.

68
00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:41.900
So let's start with software design.

69
00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.260
Software design includes the compiler,

70
00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:47.780
firmware, operating system, applications.

71
00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:50.580
The hardware design includes physical, circuit,

72
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.260
digital design, data path control,

73
00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:56.980
instruction set procedures, and IO systems.

74
00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:59.100
Let's start by talking about the software.

75
00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.380
Now, you can generally split software

76
00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:02.900
into two distinct flavors.

77
00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:06.620
There is application software and the system software.

78
00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:09.940
Application software is what you generally call an app,

79
00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:11.660
short for application.

80
00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.140
You start it, it runs, it does its thing.

81
00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:15.900
And when it's done and you don't need it anymore,

82
00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:17.940
you just stop it and it goes away.

83
00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.060
It generally interacts with you,

84
00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.300
or maybe another user or a file,

85
00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:25.020
but it typically sits on top of the operating system.

86
00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:27.300
And it's going to use the operating system

87
00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.060
to get the data it needs, to access memory,

88
00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.580
to talk to the network, all that kind of stuff.

89
00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.020
Now, system software is the code that runs

90
00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:38.620
as part of the operating system, generally.

91
00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:39.860
Without the system software,

92
00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.780
you wouldn't be able to run any sort of application software.

93
00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.620
Because generally, it's the application software

94
00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:49.180
telling the system software what it wants.

95
00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:50.900
And then there's firmware.

96
00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:54.140
Firmware generally gets written to read-only memory.

97
00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.220
And it comes from the factory associated

98
00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.420
with that piece of hardware.

99
00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:58.660
And you're not going to change it

100
00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.020
unless there's like a problem that needs to get fixed,

101
00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:02.540
or there's a new feature coming out.

102
00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:04.820
You can upgrade the firmware to get that.

103
00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:07.140
The firmware provides a common set of methods

104
00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.620
for doing things, that the operating system is going to use.

105
00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:13.500
Let's take a step back and see what we have so far, right?

106
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.180
Because we've talked about a lot of stuff.

107
00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.940
We have the application software.

108
00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:18.900
That's the app, right?

109
00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.260
And that sits on top of the operating system,

110
00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:22.900
the system software.

111
00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:25.300
But there's actually a step before that,

112
00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.340
because you have to build the application.

113
00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:30.100
So you're right the application in software language,

114
00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:33.300
like Java, C++, something like that.

115
00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:35.180
Now, I can write some code,

116
00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:36.500
and you can probably take a look at it

117
00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:38.380
and see what I'm trying to do.

118
00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:41.060
The computer itself doesn't understand this yet

119
00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:43.900
until we run it through something called a compiler.

120
00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:45.660
A compiler takes source code,

121
00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.220
which is the stuff that I write,

122
00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:50.220
and then compiles it into something machine can run.

123
00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.020
That's called object code.

124
00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:53.580
And it's a one-way direction.

125
00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:56.100
You can't take object code and then break it apart

126
00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.540
into its source code.

127
00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:58.700
It's kind of like bake in a cake.

128
00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:01.540
You can't take a cake and turn it back into its ingredients.

129
00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:03.940
It's the kind of the finished product there.

130
00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:07.580
So that's how you make an application, or a cake.

131
00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:09.700
Okay, so we've got our compiler,

132
00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:11.100
we've got an application,

133
00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.020
we've got the operating system.

134
00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.260
And then before that, before it actually hits the hardware,

135
00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.300
we've got something called the instruction set,

136
00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.700
which is part of the instruction set architecture,

137
00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:23.220
another aspect of computer architecture.

138
00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:25.060
And this is ultimately what bridges the gap

139
00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:27.340
between the software and the hardware.

140
00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:29.420
The instruction set is basically everything

141
00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:32.420
that the computer's processor is capable of doing.

142
00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:34.540
It lists out every single function it can do.

143
00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:36.700
I can compare these two numbers together,

144
00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:37.940
tell you which one's greater,

145
00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:40.380
or if they're equal, add these two numbers together,

146
00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:42.980
store this data over here, move this data over there.

147
00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:44.380
It's nothing too fancy,

148
00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:46.540
but these are the small building blocks

149
00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:50.020
that you can use to make these higher level fancy applications

150
00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:51.100
from.

151
00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:53.860
Now, ultimately everything we're doing right now

152
00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:56.380
is so that we can get something onto the processor.

153
00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:59.420
So the processor can run this application, right?

154
00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:01.340
That's why we're doing all this stuff.

155
00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:02.940
You've probably seen a processor too,

156
00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:03.820
you're thinking about this,

157
00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:07.140
it's a square chip thing with a whole bunch of metal

158
00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:08.780
pins on the bottom of it.

159
00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:10.980
Well, those metal pins aren't just for traction,

160
00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.620
they're there because they hook into the motherboard

161
00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:15.660
or the back plane of the computer,

162
00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:18.620
and we have to send electrical impulses through it.

163
00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:19.740
That's how we tell the chip,

164
00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:22.180
or the processor, what we want it to do,

165
00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:23.620
and then we want to get the answer

166
00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:25.660
of what those two numbers added together are.

167
00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:27.300
We have to do a read, so we know

168
00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:30.100
we have to know which pins to read from, right?

169
00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:31.980
There's a whole lot of stuff to understand there,

170
00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:35.220
and that's all documented in the instruction set.

171
00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:38.860
Now, you can picture a calculator as a type of computer.

172
00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:40.540
A phone is a type of computer,

173
00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:42.780
a supercomputer is a type of computer.

174
00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:44.660
Three different types of computers,

175
00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:46.500
three different architectures,

176
00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:48.820
three different instruction sets, right?

177
00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:51.380
So we need to ultimately need a compiler

178
00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:53.540
for each one of those, right?

179
00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:55.460
The blueprints for all that are contained

180
00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:56.780
within the instruction set,

181
00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:59.980
and part of the instruction set architecture.

182
00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:02.900
Even a very simple couple lines of code program,

183
00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:05.140
like add these things together,

184
00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:07.300
it has to take quite a journey to get from something

185
00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:08.700
that I can describe to something

186
00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:11.780
that can be represented electronically on a CPU.

187
00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:14.020
And then there's a whole other area of physical design

188
00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:17.260
that describes building the logic gates and physical chips.

189
00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:18.980
That falls under digital design,

190
00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:22.300
circuit implementation, and physical implementation.

191
00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.220
And believe me, that goes off pretty far

192
00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:25.580
in its own direction.

193
00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:26.580
We've covered a lot here,

194
00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:29.340
from stuff you can see to stuff you run,

195
00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:31.700
to stuff you're right, to stuff that you,

196
00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:33.500
stuff that you just kind of have to trust that it's there

197
00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:36.300
because there's no way a computer would work without it.

198
00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.700
And there's definitely a near infinite number of ways

199
00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:40.460
you could architect a computer.

200
00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:42.420
Hopefully the computer you're using right now

201
00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:44.340
was built for a specific purpose,

202
00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:46.340
and it does it really, really well.

203
00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:48.460
In the next lesson, we're gonna talk about a computer

204
00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:50.100
that's been architected specifically

205
00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:51.820
to run business transactions.

206
00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:53.140
And that's the IBM Z.
